1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
A tool definition only pays off inside the tool use loop, where Claude calls and your code answers.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Get the definition sloppy and it's where things break.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Wrong tool, bad arguments, a call that never comes.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,000
This lesson covers that round trip, the tool choice options, the pre-typed client tools,

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,000
and the authoring mistakes to avoid.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Before we trace the loop, let's name the three pieces that make it work.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,000
First, the tool use block.

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
That's what Claude returns when it decides to call a tool, and it carries three things,

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
an ID, the name of the tool, and an input object with the arguments.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Second, the tool result block.

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
That's what your code returns after you run the tool, handing the output back so Claude can keep going.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Third, the tool use ID.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
That's the ID that links a tool result to the specific tool, use it answers.

14
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
So Claude knows which call your output belongs to.

15
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Tool use, tool result, and the ID that ties them together.

16
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Those three thread the whole conversation.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Now let's walk the round trip, and it's four steps.

18
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Step one, you send your request to Claude along with your tool definitions, so it knows

19
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,000
what's available.

20
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Step two, if Claude decides to use a tool, it comes back with a stop reason of tool use,

21
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
and it includes the tool name and the input object it wants you to run.

22
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Step three, your code takes that input, actually executes the tool, and returns a tool result

23
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
carrying the output.

24
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Step four, Claude reads that result and uses it to answer the user, or to decide on its next tool call.

25
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Send with tools, get tool use back, run it, and return the result, then Claude answers.

26
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Four steps, and your code drives two of them.

27
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Let's get the mechanics of returning a result exactly right, because small mistakes here cause errors.

28
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
When you send a result back, you wrap it in a tool result block.

29
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,000
A couple of details matter.

30
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
First, every tool result carries a tool use ID, and it has to match the ID of the call it answers.

31
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
That's how Claude connects your answer to the right request.

32
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Second, all of your results go inside a single user message, not spread across several.

33
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:25,000
And if Claude asked for more than one tool in that turn, you include a result for every one of them.

34
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Leave a tool use unanswered, and the API rejects the whole turn.

35
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:35,000
So account for all of them, match the IDs, and pack them into one message.

36
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
One more capability to understand is parallel tool use, and it's on by default.

37
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
When Claude sees several independent things it needs, it can emit multiple tool use blocks

38
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
in a single message, rather than going one at a time.

39
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
That's efficient, but it changes how you respond.

40
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:58,000
You gather the results from all of those calls and return them together, as multiple tool

41
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,000
result blocks inside one user message.

42
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
There's a subtle case to know, too.

43
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,000
If a server tool ends up grouped in parallel with a client tool, the whole turn falls back

44
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
to stop reason tool use, so you handle it just like a client call.

45
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Keep that in mind when you mix the two, otherwise parallel calls are a straightforward speed win.

46
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
You can steer whether and how Claude calls a tool with a parameter called tool choice,

47
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
and it has four values.

48
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
The first is auto.

49
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Claude decides on its own whether to use a tool, and this is the default whenever you provide tools.

50
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,000
The second is any.

51
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
This forces Claude to call one of your tools, though it doesn't say which one.

52
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,000
The third is tool.

53
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,000
This forces one specific named tool for when you know exactly the call you need.

54
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
The fourth is none.

55
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:54,000
No tool gets called at all, and that's the default when you haven't provided any tools.

56
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Auto, any tool, and none.

57
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Four values, and knowing them keeps you from inventing a fifth that doesn't exist.

58
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Let's contrast the relaxed option with the forcing ones, because they behave differently

59
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
in a way that surprises people.

60
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
With auto, Claude chooses whether and which tool to call, and it can also speak in plain

61
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,000
prose before it calls, or instead of calling.

62
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
That's the natural, flexible mode.

63
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,000
With any or tool, you're forcing a call.

64
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
The API prefills the assistance turn to make the tool happen, which means Claude emits

65
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:33,000
no natural language text before the tool use block.

66
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
So if you force a call, you don't get commentary alongside it.

67
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:43,000
If you want both a forced call and some prose, the move is to stay on auto and add an explicit

68
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:48,000
instruction in your message telling Claude to use the tool.

69
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
There's a category of tools worth calling out, because their location trips people up.

70
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Anthropic defines the schema for a handful of client tools, so you don't write the definition yourself.

71
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Bash, text editor, memory, and computer use all come pre-typed.

72
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Here's the catch.

73
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Pre-typed doesn't mean anthropic runs them.

74
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
These are client tools, which means they execute in your application, on your side.

75
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Claude returns a tool use block just like with any custom tool, and your code is what

76
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:23,000
actually runs the command or edits the file, then returns the tool result.

77
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
So don't assume a built-in schema means server execution.

78
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,000
The schema is provided, but the running is still yours to do.

79
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Let's name the three authoring mistakes that cause most tool problems.

80
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,000
The first is vague descriptions, the number one culprit, and you already know the fix

81
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
from the routing lesson.

82
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:47,000
A thin description misroutes, so Claude either skips the tool or fires it on the wrong request.

83
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,000
The second is overbroad schemas.

84
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:55,000
When your types are loose and your strings are wide open, you invite bad arguments, and

85
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,000
the model fills them with invented or malformed values.

86
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,000
The third is missing required fields.

87
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,000
If a field is genuinely essential, but you didn't mark it required, the model is free

88
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,000
to leave it out, and the call arrives incomplete.

89
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Vague descriptions, overbroad schemas, missing required fields.

90
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Fix those three, and most tool failures go away.

91
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
Here's a technique for the tools that are hard to describe.

92
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:27,000
When a parameter is genuinely complex, add a couple of input examples to the definition.

93
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
An example shows the model what a valid, well-formed call actually looks like, which

94
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,000
is often clearer than another paragraph of prose.

95
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Examples are especially good at clarifying which combinations of fields make sense together,

96
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
something a schema by itself can't express.

97
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
One rule to keep in mind, every example you provide has to validate against the schema

98
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
or the request is rejected outright.

99
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:58,000
So for a tool with an unusual or nested argument shape, one solid worked example often teaches

100
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:03,000
the model more than a long written explanation ever would.

101
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Let's bring the whole lesson together into what a tool Claude calls, well actually looks like.

102
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:13,000
It starts with a precise description, one that says not just what the tool does, but

103
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
when to use it and when to reach for something else.

104
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:23,000
It pairs that with a tight schema, narrow types, and enums instead of loose, open strings.

105
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:27,000
And it marks only the genuinely essential fields as required.

106
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:32,000
So the model isn't forced to stop and ask for things it could reasonably default.

107
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:37,000
Get those three right, the description, the schema, and the required list, and the misroutes

108
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:43,000
and malformed arguments that plague sloppy definitions mostly just disappear.

109
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:47,000
With clear intent in, clean calls out.

110
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Let's frame the big picture.

111
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
When you write a tool definition, you're really setting up a clean round trip.

112
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,000
Claude returns tool use, your code returns tool result.

113
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:03,000
What to get right is a precise description, a tight schema, and only the essential fields required.

114
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:08,000
The trap is assuming a built-in schema like Bash means Anthropic runs it.

115
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:13,000
And that wraps up writing tool definitions Claude will actually call.

116
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:14,000
I'll catch you in the next one.

